      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C10"],
      "claim": "NY0128, a guanidine-containing compound, penetrated the BBB and accumulated in rat brain, showing that a guanidine group alone does not preclude CNS access; however, its highly lipophilic triphenylmethane scaffold is not shared by the query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Pitolisant, a basic piperidine ether, showed good oral absorption and brain penetration in rodents, supporting that basic amines can cross the BBB when combined with lipophilic aromatic groups; the query lacks such a lipophilic domain.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C05"],
      "claim": "THIP (or the piperidine amino acid record) crossed the BBB rapidly, indicating that some ionized small molecules can access the CNS; the query's dicationic guanidine-azocane is more polar and lacks the transporter-recognized amino acid motif.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is guanethidine, a dicationic guanidine-azocane with very low passive permeability and high efflux liability. The closest polar basic amine evidence (C09) shows inadequate brain uptake, and the permeable guanidine example (C10) is not transferable because it relies on a large lipophilic triphenylmethane moiety. Therefore, meaningful CNS access is unlikely.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental BBB record for guanethidine itself; the closest guanidine-containing permeable record (C10) has a very different lipophilic scaffold.",
